# A folder pasted onto a folder of the same name is merged, not settled wholesale.
#
# Everything here is real: the plan, the expansion, the per-path map, the executor. What a scripted
# run cannot do is click, so the dialog is answered through the scripted prompt — but the rows it is
# answering are the rows a person would see, which is what the assert-conflict-* assertions check.

sandbox
tree .
refresh

# --- the tree ---
#
# photos\ and Backup\photos\ overlap in two places and differ everywhere else:
#
#   photos\2024\a.jpg    clashes, different content
#   photos\2024\b.jpg    clashes, byte-identical
#   photos\2024\c.jpg    no twin
#   photos\notes.txt     no twin, one level up
#   Backup\photos\old\   only on the destination side, and must survive

mkdir Backup/photos/2024
mkdir Backup/photos/old
write-text Backup/photos/2024/a.jpg destination a
write-text Backup/photos/old/keep.txt untouched
write-text photos/2024/a.jpg incoming a
write-text photos/2024/c.jpg incoming c
write-text photos/notes.txt incoming notes

# Written last and with identical content, so both sides carry the same size and — within the
# two-second granularity the rule allows for — the same timestamp.
write-text Backup/photos/2024/b.jpg same b
write-text photos/2024/b.jpg same b
refresh

# --- what the dialog asks ---
#
# Two rows, not one. The folder itself is never asked about: that is the whole change. Rows are
# named by their path relative to the drop, because after a merge two of them can share a leaf name.

conflict-plan copy photos to Backup
assert-conflict-rows 2
assert-conflict-row photos/2024/a.jpg
assert-conflict-row photos/2024/b.jpg

# The identical pair starts on Skip, and says why. Nothing else in the folder is a question at all.
assert-conflict-default photos/2024/b.jpg=skip
assert-conflict-default photos/2024/a.jpg=keep-both

dialog conflicts 01-merge-conflicts
theme dark-plus
dialog conflicts 02-merge-conflicts-dark
theme light-plus

# --- answered per row, and carried out ---
#
# Replace on a copy is new. It goes through ConflictResolution.Overwrite, which stages what it
# displaced rather than destroying it — which is what makes the paste undoable below.

conflict photos/2024/a.jpg=replace, photos/2024/b.jpg=skip
copy photos to Backup
assert-conflicts 1

# Replaced: the incoming copy took the name.
assert-exists Backup/photos/2024/a.jpg

# Merged in without a question, because nothing was in the way.
assert-exists Backup/photos/2024/c.jpg
assert-exists Backup/photos/notes.txt

# The destination kept what the source never had. A wholesale Replace would have moved this whole
# folder into staging; a merge leaves it exactly where it was.
assert-exists Backup/photos/old/keep.txt

# A copy adds without removing, so every source file is still where it started.
assert-exists photos/2024/a.jpg
assert-exists photos/2024/c.jpg

shot 03-merged

# --- and it can be taken back ---
#
# A paste was never undoable before, because a copy that displaced something kept no record of it.
# A merged one does: the shell holds the outcome, so Ctrl+Z removes what was written and restores
# what was replaced.

assert-can-undo
undo
assert-missing Backup/photos/2024/c.jpg
assert-missing Backup/photos/notes.txt
assert-exists Backup/photos/old/keep.txt

echo merged a folder, asked about two files, and put the paste back
